we've just been sent out a joint  statement from ursula von lyon  uh and president prime minister boris  johnson i should say  and i'm just going to read it out so  everybody's fully up to speed it says in  a phone call today  on the ongoing negotiations between the  european union and the united kingdom  we welcome the fact that progress has  been achieved in many areas  nevertheless significant differences  remain on three critical issues  the level playing field governance and  fisheries  both sides underlined that no agreement  is feasible  if these issues are not resolved whilst  recognizing the seriousness of these  differences we agreed that a further  effort  should be undertaken by our negotiating  teams to assess  whether they can be resolved we are  therefore instructing our chief  negotiators to reconvene  tomorrow in brussels and we will speak  again  on monday evening so that's very  interesting we we of course saw  michelle barnier returning to brussels  on friday night after those talks  broke down but it appears that boris  johnson and ursula wonderlion  have decided that it is worth trying to  get those negotiating heads back  together to see if they can thrash it  out and have another  talk about it all on monday so let's  bring adam back in  uh to analyze all of that and you can  see joe pike in westminster for us as  well  um adam it is these three sticking  points from what we know  of the three what really appears to be  the problem here  well that is the issue frankly is that  is one thing to say right go back and  carry on talking they've been talking  for literally  months and months now and there has been  precious little progress in three areas  uh those  areas are about what's called the level  playing field these are competition  rules designed to ensure  that companies for one of a better  expression companies in one country  don't get a march of companies in  another through things like state aid or  by differences  on on workers rights that's one thing  another one is governance  in other words if there is a problem  with this agreement how is that resolved  and a third very symbolic if not  economically important  is fishing and that one has been pretty  intractable because it is seen as so  symbolically important for governments  in the united kingdom and also  specifically  in france but also in the netherlands  and belgium uh and other countries  and frankly unless there is some new  mechanism which is going to  unpick these it is quite hard to see how  progress is going to be made simply uh  by ursula fonda line and boris johnson  saying go on go away and have another  couple of  days of trying to find a deal it's  possible but let's be honest they've had  months and gone  pretty much nowhere what i suspect is  going to happen is is that there are  going to have to be further negotiations  behind the scenes  there is going to have to be more  instruction certainly from the european  side  to michelle barnier about what  compromises he can make i  imagine the same with with boris johnson  and lord frost  his negotiator i understand that both  michelle barnier and lord frost were  sitting in the rooms listening in  on those phone calls that is what is  going to happen now  i'm hearing that these talks will  reconvene here in  brussels and i would say that that  actually a senior eu diplomat said to me  earlier  that he thought that there was a bit of  theatrics going on that it needed  a whack but his prediction was that you  could have a couple of days of talks  that focus would be increased because of  the looming debate around the internal  market bill  in the uk and and he thought that it was  possible that a deal could be done  by monday evening interesting that that  is when ursula fonda line and boris  johnson have said that they will talk  again frankly there is going to have to  be something going on behind the scenes  just putting these negotiators back  together and asking them to try again  i don't think will work sure okay adam  well let's bring in joe pike in  westminster given what adam's saying  there joe  do you think that we have reason to be  pessimistic that a deal can be reached  i think there this uh statement  leaves us feeling maybe a bit more  optimistic than we certainly started the  day  isabel because of course we knew that  this call was significant and would  probably steer us  towards the eventual conclusion that  certainly doesn't seem to be no deal  at this stage interesting that this  talks are starting so  uh fast again they will start tomorrow  but that  both leaders boris johnson and ursula  underline will talk on monday so i  suppose  uh the negotiators are being given a day  a day and a half to see if they can make  any progress perhaps  based on that private conversation today  between their  two bosses or the leaders will get  involved yet again certainly downing  street were very optimistic  towards the end of this week but  yesterday things changed they believe  that new demands were placed on them by  michelle barnier new demands that they  think were coordinated by  france by spain perhaps by italy as well  and new demands not just on fishing but  on  state a they were pretty frustrated  about that and uh some in government  think there is absolutely no chance that  they will  commit to any of the demands of course  david frost as adam said there is with  boris johnson at checkers that's where  the prime minister took that call and  i'm sure they'll be discussing now  where to go next certainly conservative  mps seems to be  a pretty supportive of boris johnson in  private and in public i think in  contrast  it's ursula von de line who after uh  many months of saying that  the eu would speak as one now has the  far more difficult task  of trying to keep eu leaders focused  and trying to get them to commit to a  deal kate joe pike and westminster adam  parsons in brussels thank you both very  much  indeed for